\begin_layout Subsection
Minimal Coupling and the Stationary Equation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The time-dependent wave equation is 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
i\hbar\frac{\partial\Psi(\mathbf r,t)}{\partial t}
=
\hat H\Psi(\mathbf r,t),
\label{eq:app_time_dependent_wave_equation}
\end{equation}
\end_inset

with the minimal-coupling Hamiltonian 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\hat H
=
\sqrt{
c^2\left(\hat{\mathbf p}-q_e\mathbf A/c\right)^2+m_0^2c^4
}
+
q_eV(\mathbf r),
\label{eq:app_minimal_coupling_hamiltonian}
\end{equation}
\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\mathbf p}=-i\hbar\nabla$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf A$
\end_inset

 is the magnetic vector potential, and 
\begin_inset Formula $V(\mathbf r)$
\end_inset

 is the specimen electrostatic potential in volts. We use 
\begin_inset Formula $e>0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $q_e=-e$
\end_inset

, so the electron potential energy is 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
E_{\mathrm{pot}}(\mathbf r)
=
q_eV(\mathbf r)
=
-eV(\mathbf r).
\label{eq:app_electron_potential_energy}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This notation distinguishes the electrostatic potential 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset

 from the energy 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{\mathrm{pot}}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Neglecting magnetic interactions and introducing the rest energy 
\begin_inset Formula $ E_0 = m_0 c^2$
\end_inset

 gives 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\hat H
=
\sqrt{c^2\hat{\mathbf p}^{\,2}+m_0^2c^4}
+
E_{\mathrm{pot}}(\mathbf r) = \sqrt{c^2\hat{\mathbf p}^{\,2}+E_0^2}
+
E_{\mathrm{pot}}(\mathbf r)
\label{eq:app_relativistic_hamiltonian}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We then look for stationnary states 
\begin_inset Formula $\Psi(\mathbf r,t)$
\end_inset

 that separate the temporal and spatial contributions : 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\Psi(\mathbf r,t)
=
\psi(\mathbf r)
\exp\left(-\frac{iE_{\mathrm{tot}}t}{\hbar}\right),
\label{eq:app_stationary_ansatz}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The stationary equation is therefore 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\hat H\psi=E_{\mathrm{tot}}\psi.
\label{eq:app_stationary_equation}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula $E_{\mathrm{tot}}$
\end_inset

 is the conserved total relativistic energy, including the rest energy. As we look for elastic interaction this energy is conserved. In particular, in vacuum, 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
E_{\mathrm{tot}}
=
\gamma m_0c^2
=
\sqrt{c^2p_0^2+E_0^2},
\qquad
p_0=hk_0=\frac{h}{\lambda},
\label{eq:app_vacuum_energy_momentum}
\end{equation}
\end_inset

. If the beam has accelerated through a voltage magnitude 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{\mathrm{acc}}>0$
\end_inset

, the total energy is the sum of the rest and kinetic energies :
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
and 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{\mathrm{kin}}=eU_{\mathrm{acc}}=E_{\mathrm{tot}}-E_0 = (\gamma - 1) E_0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Equation
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:app_vacuum_energy_momentum"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 can be used to compute the wave length : 
\begin_inset Formula $c^2 p_0^2 = h^2 c^2/\lambda^2 = E_{\mathrm{tot}}^2 - E_0^2 = (E_{\mathrm{kin}}+E_0)^2 - E_0^2$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Klein–Gordon and Corrected Schrödinger Forms
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Rearranging Eq.
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:app_stationary_equation"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 and squaring gives 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\left[c^2\hat{\mathbf p}^{\,2}+E_0^2\right]\psi
=
\left[E_{\mathrm{tot}}-E_{\mathrm{pot}}(\mathbf r)\right]^2\psi .
\label{eq:app_kg_before_linearisation}
\end{equation}
\end_inset

A local relattivistic momentum and a local wavelength can be defined : 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
c^2\hat{\mathbf p}^{\,2}
=
\left[E_{\mathrm{tot}}-E_{\mathrm{pot}}(\mathbf r)\right]^2 - E_0^2 = h^2 c^2 \mathbf k^2
\label{eq:app_kg_before_linearisation2}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Using 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\mathbf p}^{\,2}=-\hbar^2\nabla^2$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p_0=h k_0$
\end_inset

, equation 
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:app_vacuum_energy_momentum"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 becomes 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\nabla^2\psi(\mathbf r)
+
(2\pi k_0)^2n_{\mathrm{KG}}^2(\mathbf r)\psi(\mathbf r)
=0,
\label{eq:app_kg_helmholtz_form}
\end{equation}
\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
n_{\mathrm{KG}}^2(\mathbf r)
=
\frac{
\left[E_{\mathrm{tot}}-E_{\mathrm{pot}}(\mathbf r)\right]^2-E_0^2
} {
E_{\mathrm{tot}}^2-E_0^2
} = \frac{p(\mathbf r)}{p_0}=\frac{k(\mathbf r)}{k_0}
.
\label{eq:app_kg_refractive_index}
\end{equation}
\end_inset

So, 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{\mathrm{KG}}$
\end_inset

 is the ratio of the local relativistic momentum 
\begin_inset Formula $p(r)$
\end_inset

 to the vacuum momentum 
\begin_inset Formula $p_0$
\end_inset

 . This connects the Klein–Gordon form to the electron-optical refractive-index concepts of Glaser and Ehrenberg–Siday 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
key "Glaser1933,Ehrenberg-Siday1949"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For 
\begin_inset Formula $|E_{\mathrm{pot}}|\ll E_{\mathrm{tot}}$
\end_inset

, as it is the case for electron-beam interaction, 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\left[E_{\mathrm{tot}}-E_{\mathrm{pot}}(\mathbf r)\right]^2
\simeq
E_{\mathrm{tot}}^2
-
2E_{\mathrm{tot}}E_{\mathrm{pot}}(\mathbf r).
\label{eq:app_linearised_energy}
\end{equation}
\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{\mathrm{tot}}/c^2=\gamma m_0$
\end_inset

, Eq.
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:app_kg_before_linearisation"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 then reduces to 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\nabla^2\psi(\mathbf r)
+
\left[
(2\pi k_0)^2
-
\frac{2\gamma m_0}{\hbar^2}E_{\mathrm{pot}}(\mathbf r)
\right]\psi(\mathbf r)
=0.
\label{eq:app_corrected_schrodinger}
\end{equation}
\end_inset

or equivalently ,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\left[
-\frac{\hbar^2}{2\gamma m_0}\nabla^2
+
E_{\mathrm{pot}}(\mathbf r)
\right]\psi(\mathbf r)
=
\frac{p_0^2}{2\gamma m_0}\psi(\mathbf r).
\label{eq:app_schrodinger_energy_form}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Equation
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:app_schrodinger_energy_form"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 is therefore a stationary Schrödinger-like reformulation of the relativistic wave equation, rather than the original relativistic energy-eigenvalue equation. This is the relativistically corrected Schrödinger form as deduced by Fujiwara. The right-hand side resembles the nonrelativistic kinetic energy 
\begin_inset Formula $p_0^2/(2m)$
\end_inset

, with the rest mass replaced by the relativistic mass 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma m_0$
\end_inset

. It should not, however, be identified with either the total relativistic energy 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{\mathrm{tot}}$
\end_inset

 or, in general, the kinetic energy 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{\mathrm{kin}}=E_{\mathrm{tot}}-E_0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Indeed, using 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\gamma m_0=\frac{E_{\mathrm{tot}}}{c^2},
\qquad
p_0^2c^2=E_{\mathrm{tot}}^2-E_0^2,
\end{equation}
\end_inset

the eigenvalue in Eq.
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:app_schrodinger_energy_form"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 becomes 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\frac{p_0^2}{2\gamma m_0}
=
\frac{E_{\mathrm{tot}}^2-E_0^2}{2E_{\mathrm{tot}}}
=
E_{\mathrm{kin}}\frac{E_{\mathrm{tot}}+E_0}{2E_{\mathrm{tot}}}.
\label{eq:app_effective_schrodinger_eigenvalue}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It approaches 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{\mathrm{kin}}$
\end_inset

 only in the nonrelativistic limit 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{\mathrm{kin}}\ll E_0$
\end_inset

, for which 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{\mathrm{tot}}\simeq E_0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Equation xxxx-mettrelabonneREF indicates that the energy appearing in the temporal dependence of the wave function is not 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{0\mathrm{kin}}$
\end_inset

 but 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{\mathrm{tot}}$
\end_inset

. The factor 
\begin_inset Formula $1/2$
\end_inset

 arises when the scalar relativistic wave equation is linearised in the specimen potential and divided by 
\begin_inset Formula $2E_{\mathrm{tot}}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Interaction Constant and Paraxial Limit
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The conventional TEM interaction constant is 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\sigma
=
\frac{2\pi\gamma m_0e}{h^2k_0}
=
\frac{2\pi\gamma m_0e\lambda}{h^2}= 2 \pi e  k_0 \frac{\gamma m_0 c^2}{h^2 k_0^2 c^2}=
\frac{2\pi e E_{\mathrm{tot}}}
{hc\sqrt{E_{\mathrm{tot}}^2-E_0^2}}.
\label{eq:app_sigma_definition}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The potential term in Eq.
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:app_corrected_schrodinger"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 can therefore be written as 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\frac{2\gamma m_0e}{\hbar^2}V(\mathbf r)
=
4\pi k_0\sigma V(\mathbf r),
\label{eq:app_potential_term_sigma}
\end{equation}
\end_inset

giving 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\nabla^2\psi(\mathbf r)
+
\left[
(2\pi k_0)^2
+
4\pi k_0\sigma V(\mathbf r)
\right]\psi(\mathbf r)
=0.
\label{eq:app_corrected_schrodinger_sigma}
\end{equation}
\end_inset

Factoring out the carrier wave, 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\psi(\mathbf r)
=
\exp(2\pi i k_0z)\tilde\psi(\mathbf r),
\label{eq:app_carrier_wave}
\end{equation}
\end_inset

and neglecting 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial^2\tilde\psi/\partial z^2$
\end_inset

 gives 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\frac{\partial\tilde\psi}{\partial z}
=
\frac{i}{4\pi k_0}\nabla_\perp^2\tilde\psi
+
i\sigma V(\mathbf r)\tilde\psi.
\label{eq:app_paraxial_sigma}
\end{equation}
\end_inset

For a slice with projected electrostatic potential 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
V_j(x,y)
=
\int_{z_j}^{z_j+\Delta z}V(x,y,z)\,dz,
\label{eq:app_projected_potential}
\end{equation}
\end_inset

the phase-grating transmission function is 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
t_j(x,y)
=
\exp\left[i\sigma V_j(x,y)\right],
\label{eq:app_transmission_function}
\end{equation}
\end_inset

which is the expression used in the main text.
\end_layout

